course: dictionary
lesson: 8900
title: One word, many jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. The word shuck is a good example. «We shuck the corn on the board before we cook it.» Here shuck means pulling the outer covering off, so the corn can be eaten. Ask: which meaning fits this sentence?
explain: Shuck can also mean taking off clothes quickly. «He shucked off his wet coat and sat by the fire.» The word off is a clue: the coat came away from him. Try each meaning in the sentence, and only the one that makes sense is right.
explain: Be careful when a word sounds like a word you know. In Ireland, «the Taoiseach (said TEE-shuck) runs the government» sounds a bit like shuck, but the sentence is about a leader, not corn or coats. Sound alone does not tell you the meaning, so check the context.
explain: Shucks is an old American word, not a swear word. When someone praises you, you might say «Aw, shucks, thanks!» This shows you feel a bit shy or embarrassed. The words and feelings around it are your clue.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Shuck» means to remove the outer covering, like the husk of corn or the shell of an oyster.
fact: «Shuck off» means to take off clothes or get rid of something quickly.
fact: «Shucks» (US, old-fashioned) shows mild disappointment or shyness at praise.
fact: «Taoiseach» is said TEE-shuck, but it means the leader of the government in Ireland. It has nothing to do with corn or coats.
exercise: «We shuck the corn before we boil it.» What does shuck mean here? | To eat it raw | *To take the outer covering off | To wash it in the sink | To cook it in hot water
exercise: «He shucked off his muddy boots at the door.» What does shucked off mean? | To paint them red | To polish them until they shine | *To pull them off quickly | To lace them up tight
exercise: Mum says, «Aw, shucks, thanks for helping!» What does shucks show? | A question about the weather | A loud shout of joy | Anger about a broken plate | *A shy, mild reaction to praise
test: «The chef will shuck the oysters before the guests arrive.» What is the chef doing to the oysters? | *Opening them by removing the shell | Counting them | Selling them | Cooking them
test: Ella says Taoiseach sounds like shuck, so it must mean taking off a coat. Why is she wrong? | Taoiseach is a kind of corn | Taoiseach is a type of oyster | Taoiseach means a coat in Irish | *The sound is similar, but the sentence is about a leader, not clothes
exam: «Before supper, Grandma sat on the porch to shuck a big pile of corn.» What does shuck mean in this sentence? | To count the pile | To paint the porch | *To remove the husks | To sing a song
